WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.465
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.657 --> 00:00:05.490
STEREN GIANNINI: Hi.

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:06.540
I'm Steren.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.120
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ:
And I'm Karolina.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:09.620
STEREN GIANNINI:
Today, we are going

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.650
to talk about how to run
your jobs on surveillance

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:14.780
with Cloud Run.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.680
First, let me recap
what Cloud Run is.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:22.520
Cloud Run allows you to
deploy and scale applications

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:27.260
fast and securely in a
fully managed environment.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.030
Let's jump into a quick demo.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:33.290
I will show you how easy
it is to deploy your Python

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:37.520
app to Cloud Run from
a GitHub repository.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.900
From the Cloud Console, I
navigate to the Cloud Run page.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.760
I create a service.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.390
I select "Continuously deploy
from a source repository."

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:51.990
The panel opens where
I can connect to GitHub

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:55.690
and select one of
my Git repositories.

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:57.610
My repo is a Python application.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:00.680
It doesn't include
any Docker file.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:05.760
Cloud Run can build from sources
from many popular languages.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.030
For the sake of the demo, I
will make my service public

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:12.240
by allowing unauthenticated
invocations.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.050
And I click Deploy.

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:20.840
My code is built into a
container image by Cloud Build

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:24.760
and then will be
deployed to Cloud Run.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.620
If we take a quick
look at the code,

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:30.880
we can see it is a standard
Python flask application.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:33.640
I used a requirement
that takes the file

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:35.320
to declare my dependencies.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:39.100
And there is no Docker file.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:43.780
Once completed, I get a stable
and secure HTTPS endpoint

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:46.590
to invoke my service.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:49.110
Cloud Run will start
as many instances

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:54.510
as needed to handle all incoming
traffic, including scale to 0.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:57.015
Let's give it a try
by providing an image.

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:02.290
We can see that our
microservice has

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:06.160
transformed my picture of Half
Dome into a coloring book.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.750
Nice.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:11.120
Back into the user
interface, I can see which

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:13.910
Git commit is actually running.

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:15.860
When I push to my
Git repository,

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.440
my code will
automatically be built

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:22.950
and new revisions of this
service will be deployed.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:27.680
I can see at the top a
build history at a glance.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.230
I can see key metrics of my
service and my service's logs.

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:38.240
I can quickly edit
settings of my service,

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:41.720
like environment variables,
mounting Secrets,

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:44.720
connecting to Cloud SQL.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:46.580
Let's select More
Memory in order

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:48.770
to be able to process
bigger images.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.770
By default, these new
settings will roll out

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.080
as soon as I click Deploy.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:02.370
But let's do something different
and not serve this new revision

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.480
immediately.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:09.210
Once deployed, this new revision
is serving 0% of the traffic.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:13.020
I can test it using a
dedicated URL endpoint.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.900
And then I can gradually
roll out traffic

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.900
to it-- let's say 10%.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.990
If anything goes wrong,
I can always roll back

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:25.350
to a previous revision.

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:27.600
So hopefully, this
demo shows you

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:31.290
how easy it is to deploy
an app from a GitHub repo

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:33.340
to Cloud Run.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:36.940
Cloud Run allows you to
deploy and scale applications

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:41.290
fast and securely in a
fully managed environment.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:45.100
As you've seen, it is
simple and automated.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:48.040
You have no infrastructure
to provision in advance,

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:50.020
no cluster to manage.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:55.570
And it allows you to be more
productive, to move faster.

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:59.300
So when should
you use Cloud Run?

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:03.870
We identified three
big categories--

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:08.610
first, websites and APIs.

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:12.180
This goes from traditional
server-side rendered web pages

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:15.540
to REST or GraphQL APIs.

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:20.190
But Cloud Run also has built-in
support for web sockets,

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:25.020
allowing you to stream data
between front end and back end.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:30.040
Then private
services-- for example,

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:33.270
you can put a website behind
an authentication page

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:37.500
with Google Cloud
Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:40.890
You can build private
microservice-to-microservice

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:47.010
architectures, either
over HTP or gRPC.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:50.580
And last, data processing--

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:54.540
Cloud Run services can be
invoked by Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:57.210
can receive tasks
from Cloud Tasks,

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:00.510
and receive events
from Eventarc.

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:04.110
Cloud Run automatically
scales the number of instances

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:07.840
of your code, even down to 0.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:11.020
If your app suddenly
gets a burst of traffic,

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:13.030
there is nothing to worry about.

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:18.760
Cloud Run will scale fast to
handle all incoming requests.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:21.550
And when fewer
instances are needed,

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:25.900
Cloud Run will automatically
reduce their number down to 0

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:29.830
if no traffic is
received after a while.

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:33.130
But how is Cloud Run priced?

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:37.690
Well, first, Cloud
Run has a free tier.

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:40.300
Each month, you get
2 million requests

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:43.560
available at no charge.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:47.880
Then Cloud Run truly
is pay per views.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:53.940
You pay for the exact CPU and
memory used by your services

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:55.770
when they are
processing requests

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:59.040
with 100-millisecond
granularity.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:04.200
That means that there is no
charge if it's not in use.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:08.955
As we've seen, we can deploy
source code written in Node.js,

00:06:08.955 --> 00:06:14.100
Python, Go, .Net,
Kotlin, Scala, Java,

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:17.940
and any other language
via containers.

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:21.770
Container images are,
basically, like a ZIP file.

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:23.930
They allow you to
package everything

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:26.300
that's needed to run your code.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:29.190
This, of course, includes
its language packages,

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:32.210
but also any native
libraries or custom

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:34.850
binaries that you would need.

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:38.740
And finally, I want to highlight
how Cloud Run integrates

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:41.200
with Firebase.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:44.920
Your code on Cloud Run can
query data from Firestore

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:49.340
or use Firebase
clients or admin SDKs.

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:52.400
But you can also
use Firebase Hosting

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:54.650
in front of your
Cloud Run service

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:58.490
in order to host
static files or assets,

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:02.600
to cache your HTTP requests
using the cache control header,

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:07.320
or to easily set up
your own custom domain.

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:11.237
And now let's hear
from Karolina.

00:07:11.237 --> 00:07:13.820
KAROLINA NETOLICKÁ: So now that
we've learned about Cloud Run,

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:16.840
let's talk about Cloud Run jobs.

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:20.290
Imagine you're using Cloud
Run to run an e-commerce site

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:22.000
and it's working great.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.445
Cloud run is autoscaling
your service to meet demand.

00:07:25.445 --> 00:07:27.070
You're really happy
that you don't need

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:28.900
to manage any infrastructure.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:32.170
And your developer
velocity has gone up.

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:34.090
But you find yourself
with pieces of work

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:37.450
that don't fit neatly into
any Cloud Run service.

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:40.030
For example, your
suppliers send you

00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:43.420
invoices that you need to
periodically process and pay.

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:46.280
You could write a web
service that does this task.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:48.850
But what you'd really like
to do is write a script

00:07:48.850 --> 00:07:51.520
to process all these
invoices and then

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:53.870
run the script as a cron job.

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:55.610
This is where Cloud
Run jobs come in.

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:59.000
Cloud Run jobs are a new feature
of Cloud Run that we just

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:02.230
announced here at
I/O. Cloud Run jobs

00:08:02.230 --> 00:08:05.290
let you run scripts and data
processing jobs in Cloud Run

00:08:05.290 --> 00:08:08.270
right alongside your services.

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:10.840
This means that you get to
benefit from Cloud Run's

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:14.450
simple integration with the rest
of the Google Cloud ecosystem,

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:17.560
including Secret Manager
and fine-grained IAM.

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:20.870
Cloud Run also works with
many common storage systems.

00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:23.350
So you have flexibility
on where to store any data

00:08:23.350 --> 00:08:25.820
that your job is processing.

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:27.890
What's unique about
Cloud Run jobs

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:30.110
is that they start up
much faster than starting

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:32.520
a VM to run a script on.

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:37.559
In fact, many jobs start running
in as little as 10 seconds.

00:08:37.559 --> 00:08:39.510
If your workload
is parallelizable,

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:41.789
you can further speed it
up by splitting your work

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:44.010
into multiple
tasks, each of which

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:47.070
handles a subset of your input.

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:50.010
Between the fast startup
and running in parallel,

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:52.530
Cloud Run jobs work
particularly well for situations

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:54.480
where you need jobs
to complete quickly--

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:58.150
for example, because there's a
person waiting for the result.

00:08:58.150 --> 00:09:00.330
So what are Cloud
Run jobs useful for?

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:03.570
First, they're a great match
for scheduled scripts, things

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:08.110
like nightly reports, database
maintenance, and audit scripts.

00:09:08.110 --> 00:09:10.270
You can run these
scripts on a schedule

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:11.860
by using Cloud Scheduler.

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:14.440
Cloud Run jobs are also great
for background processing,

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:17.230
where some user
interaction has resulted

00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:20.620
in offline work that needs to
be done in the background--

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:22.690
for example, sending
email campaigns for

00:09:22.690 --> 00:09:26.110
abandoned shopping carts or
running web testing suites.

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:30.930
Finally, you can use Cloud Run
jobs for batch data processing.

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:34.662
So let's go back to our previous
example of processing invoices.

00:09:34.662 --> 00:09:36.620
I'm going to show you
how you can use Cloud Run

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:40.640
jobs to process these
invoices using Document AI

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:43.670
and to store the parsed
data in Firestore.

00:09:43.670 --> 00:09:45.890
To speed up processing,
we'll run the job

00:09:45.890 --> 00:09:48.080
with eight parallel
tasks, each of which

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:50.840
will process a subset
of the invoices.

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:53.030
We'll also show a
simple web service

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:55.490
that lets us review
the processed invoices

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:58.170
and approve them for payment.

00:09:58.170 --> 00:10:01.140
Here's the Cloud Storage bucket
where our invoices are stored.

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:03.300
New ones come in
every day and are

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:06.660
placed in a folder called
Incoming to indicate that they

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:08.550
haven't been processed.

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:10.920
Right now, this folder
has a few dozen invoices

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:15.080
waiting to be processed
the next time the job runs.

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:17.070
Let's take a look
at a few of them.

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:20.030
You can see that these are
designed for humans to look at

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:22.720
rather than to be
machine-readable.

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:25.720
Here's the code we'll use
to process these invoices.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:28.600
The code loops through the
contents of the storage bucket.

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:31.300
First, it skips any
invoice that will

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:33.490
be handled by a different task.

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:37.070
Each running task is
aware of its task index.

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:40.300
So it can look up which file
name prefixes it is responsible

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:44.240
for versus other tasks
are responsible for

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:46.040
and decide whether
to skip or process

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:48.550
this particular invoice.

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:51.220
It also skips anything
that looks like a folder.

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:55.030
Then it calls
Document AI to extract

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:58.750
the invoice data, such as
vendor names and totals.

00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:01.740
And it saves that
information to Firestore.

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:04.570
Finally, it moves the
invoice to a different folder

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:06.270
so that it won't
be processed again

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:08.760
the next time the job is run.

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:10.560
Now let's build a container.

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:13.200
I'm going to use Cloud
Build from the command line

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:17.730
to create the container and
save it to a repository.

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:19.550
Now I'll create
my Cloud Run job.

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:22.880
I'll specify the image to
run, the region to run in,

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:26.030
the number of tasks, and a
few environment variables

00:11:26.030 --> 00:11:27.590
that the container needs.

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:29.550
And I have a job.

00:11:29.550 --> 00:11:32.510
Let's take a look at my list
of jobs in the Cloud Console.

00:11:32.510 --> 00:11:34.010
There it is.

00:11:34.010 --> 00:11:36.840
I'm going to click Execute.

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:38.130
And here's my job running.

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:40.920
I'm going to click on
it to get more details.

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:42.960
The progress bar
at the top is going

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:47.527
to update as tasks
start and finish.

00:11:47.527 --> 00:11:49.360
While we're waiting for
the job to complete,

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:51.700
let's take a quick
look at the logs.

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:54.770
You can see the debugging
messages right here.

00:11:54.770 --> 00:11:58.470
So you don't have
to navigate away.

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:00.000
The job succeeded.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.430
Let's take a look at the output.

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:04.770
The data that's been pulled
out from these invoices

00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:06.750
has been saved in Firestore.

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:09.600
We see here that we have
a collection of invoices.

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:12.480
And for each invoice, we can
see the amount, the company it's

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:15.350
from, the due date, and so on.

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:17.270
And to make it
even easier to see,

00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:21.170
here's a simple web page hosted
as a Cloud Run service that

00:12:21.170 --> 00:12:23.000
lets you review the
processed invoices

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:26.460
and select whether to
approve them for payment.

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:29.220
Finally, we want this
job to run every night.

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:31.830
Cloud Scheduler is
perfect for this.

00:12:31.830 --> 00:12:35.190
I'll give Cloud Scheduler
a name and a region.

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:37.275
I'm going to run my job
at 1:00 AM every night.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:43.390
My Cloud Run job will be
triggered via an HTTP request

00:12:43.390 --> 00:12:45.610
through the Cloud Run admin API.

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:49.030
You can find the URL
in the documentation.

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:52.060
As the last step, I'll give
Cloud Scheduler permissions

00:12:52.060 --> 00:12:53.650
to start the Cloud Run job.

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:00.990
The Scheduler trigger is ready.

00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:02.750
It will run tonight.

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:04.760
But let's test it once.

00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:06.020
I'll click Run Now.

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:08.980
And then I'll go take
a look at my job.

00:13:08.980 --> 00:13:11.080
I see a new execution
has already started.

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:13.840
And I already have
tasks running.

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:16.450
So let's recap what
I've shown you here.

00:13:16.450 --> 00:13:20.170
I built and deployed a Cloud
Run job, which loads invoices

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:25.000
from Cloud Storage, calls
Document AI to analyze them,

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:29.210
and stores the parsed
invoice data in Firestore.

00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:30.710
My job runs every night.

00:13:30.710 --> 00:13:35.000
And we have a nice interface for
our team to approve invoices.

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:38.090
And at the end of all of this,
we have no VMs or clusters

00:13:38.090 --> 00:13:39.470
that we need to manage.

00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:42.200
And we only pay when our
services or jobs are actually

00:13:42.200 --> 00:13:43.630
running.

00:13:43.630 --> 00:13:46.320
So to summarize
it all, Cloud Run

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:50.020
lets you run your containers
in a fully managed environment

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:52.530
so that we take care of
infrastructure management

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:55.550
and you focus on
your business logic.

00:13:55.550 --> 00:13:59.420
Use Cloud Run services for
web services and Cloud Run

00:13:59.420 --> 00:14:02.180
jobs for containers
that run to completion,

00:14:02.180 --> 00:14:07.190
such as scripts and
data processing jobs.

00:14:07.190 --> 00:14:09.590
STEREN GIANNINI: Cloud
Run is a simpler way

00:14:09.590 --> 00:14:11.930
to deploy your web services--

00:14:11.930 --> 00:14:14.110
